def _secret_get(key, default=None):
    """Read a secret, trying a few real-world places it might actually
    live before giving up — and never exploding when no secrets.toml
    exists.

    This exists because "I set the key and it's still not picked up"
    almost always turns out to be one of:
      1. The key was pasted under a [section] table in secrets.toml
         (e.g. `[ai]\\nANTHROPIC_API_KEY = "..."`) instead of at the
         top level — st.secrets["ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"] only finds a
         TOP-LEVEL key, so a nested one silently misses. Checked here
         by also scanning one level into every table.
      2. The app isn't actually reading secrets.toml at all because
         it's deployed somewhere that uses real environment variables
         instead (Render, Railway, Docker, etc.) — os.environ is
         checked as a genuine second source, not just a fallback for
         local dev.
      3. A trailing newline or space survived a copy-paste into the
         Streamlit Cloud secrets editor — technically "set", but a
         non-empty whitespace-only value would otherwise pass the
         `bool(...)` check in AI_FEATURES_AVAILABLE and then fail the
         actual API call with a confusing auth error. Stripped here
         so a whitespace-only value is treated as genuinely unset
         instead of silently "succeeding".

    (What this can't fix: Streamlit Community Cloud specifically
    caches secrets.toml at process start and needs the app manually
    **rebooted** — not just re-run — from the app's "Manage app" menu
    before a newly-added or edited secret takes effect at all. If a
    key is confirmed present in the Secrets Diagnostics panel below
    but AI features are still disabled, that reboot is the next
    thing to check, not this function.)
    """
    try:
        val = st.secrets[key]
        if isinstance(val, str):
            val = val.strip()
        if val:
            return val
    except Exception:
        pass
    # One level into any [section] table, in case the key was nested
    # rather than placed at the top level of secrets.toml.
    try:
        for _v in st.secrets.values():
            if hasattr(_v, "get"):
                _nested = _v.get(key)
                if isinstance(_nested, str):
                    _nested = _nested.strip()
                if _nested:
                    return _nested
    except Exception:
        pass
    _env_val = os.environ.get(key)
    if _env_val is not None:
        _env_val = _env_val.strip()
        if _env_val:
            return _env_val
    return default


